Just finished my first ascension in 3.1 and had a few thought about
the game.  I played the early 1.0 Hack many years ago, and just
recently picked up nethack 3.1 for my home PC.  After losing one or
two games ;^), I took Joc the wizard up to god-hood.  Skipping the
ussual statistics, I would like to relate two ideas I had for changes
to the game.  Some of the points are sketchy, but sufficiant for
discussion. 

1. Make a magic track strategy

The idea here is to allow for a winning strategy based on primairly
use of magic versus hack and slash.  Currently, once you acquire a +7
blessed rust-proof Mjollnir or dragon scale mail etc, its yours for
the life of the game.  It doesn't diminish with usage (it can be
stolen though).  But with magic, most things other then rings are
temporary - they are used for a limitied number of times.  A
replacement must be found, or the item recharged - still a temporary
solution.  I would like to see magical items be available and used
similar to that of armor and weapons, ie. long term usage.

The path is spiritual rather then material.  To do this you would have
to give up armor class and weapon use.  Chosing one path makes the
other weaker - lots of armor and weapons use, little magic; little armor
and weapons use, lots of magic.  

Here are some of the characterisitics of this type of strategy:

  - spells are not forgotten
    - ring, artifact, potion, special knowledge etc., may be required
    - re-reading of spell books may be required
    - could be based on knowledge, level, experiances

  - ability to cast multiple spells or zap multiple wands in one move
    - could put some limitations on when and how
    - could be like "final protective fire" (see who bites on that one)

  - ability to create potions, or make wands, or rings
    - simple to complicated scheme based on value of item
      - collect eye of toad, tail of newt etc., then #dip them
      - convert staffs or other weapons into wands
      - recieve from god
    - technique is based on
      - how much you know (level and discoveries)
      - collection of certain items
      - knowledge of how to do it
        - could be passed to you by your quest leader, or other
	  magician (priest, liches, shamons etc)
          - opens up whole new area for #chat
          - pay
          - coerce
        - method (ingrediants, technique?) randomized for each game so
	  you must learn new way instead of reading spoiler file.
          - I would like to see a substantial part of the early game
	    used in trying to learn this.  Each major section of game
	    (mines, quest, castle etc.) could give you more powerful abilities.
          - key is learning from clues rather then spoiler file
          - could have matrix of ingrediants, magic words, sequances, etc.
	    which are randomized each game.  Trying without knowledge
	    could have significant negative effects.
        - You don't play the game linearly.  Maybe you do part of the
	  mines, then do the quest,  do up to the medusa level, then
	  complete the mines, then the castle etc.  What promots this
	  is that you effectively need something inorder to increase
	  chance of success in the other. 
          - you might recieve advice along the way
            "My Son, it is time to seek the quest"
            "Enter not unless you have the strength (fighter) or the
	    blah blah spell"
            "what you seek is with the woman of stone"

  - Ability to do magic proportional to armor class.  Most powerful
    magician would be at say armor class of zero (0) with significant drop
    off below that - effective limit -10.  Or it could be tied to
    wearing metal armor (should also  inclued dragon scale mail).
    - You could regain magic over time once you reduce your armor class

  - Use of weapons also diminishes magic as you use the weapons 
    - Some weapons you could use with impunity
      - atheme (Magicbane?)
      - knives, daggers (Sting?)
      - rocks
    - Once you stop using the weapon you regain magic with time (in a
      tight spot I would want to be able to pull out my trusty +7.
      Mjollnir and pay the price later).

  - You could have multiple strategies - be the fighter in the early
    game, the magician in the endgame, or vice versa.

  - Balance the imposed physical weakness with a strong familiar.
    Give them a special relationship and control.  Would like to see
    balance such that a wizard can not win (via a magic strategy)
    without their familar, but wizard must protect and nurture the
    familiar as they have limited capabilities (ie they aren't gray
    dragons).
    - could make control of familiar part of magic ability, you lose
      your magic, you lose control of your familiar.
    - transfer power or energy to familiar that is hurting
    - armor your familiar
    - make them an important part of magic making
    - basicly magician does the magic, the familiar the fighting
    - could start with the ussual pets, but could end up with
      hellhound for dog, and something equal for cat.
      - need certain spell to turn them into familiar from pet (say
        its in the mine town)
    - If they are polymorphed, they just become a pet (keeps them from
      being too powerful).

2. Mercenaries or something to do with all that gold

How bout the addition of mercenaries.  You come across a horde of
Orcs.  Instead of fighting them, you buy them as merconaries.  Or the
room full of soldiers, or a legion of elves.  I would like to see
something more then just the effect of a blessed scroll of taming.  I
would like to see some sort of cooperative effort or control over
them, say with coded bugle blasts.  The following are some ideas:

- Candidates
  - Orcs
  - Soldiars
  - Elves
  - Killer bees
  - wolves

- could hire with gold and gems
  - contract for so many levels
  - must deal with NCO (officer, lord etc) or above if you want
    organized help 
  - could extend contract with more gold

- Can use bugle to control
  - fall-in
  - follow me
  - attack
  - retreat
  - rest, eat

- Can outfit them with better weapons and armor

- They will loot (mostly when you don't want them too - how do you get
  more gold to pay them if they loot it all before you?)
- They love potions of booze and can be a real pain when drunk
- They will squable amongst themselves, sometimes breaking out in
  fights which may include you.
- Another monster can buy there loyalty
- They may turn on you if hungry, tired, or not getting fair share of
  loot
- They may desert
- If their leader gets killed, they become unmanageable, ie same as a
  bunch of pet orcs
- With a scroll of taming you can gain their undying loyalty and
  devotion
- if you cheat them or kill them, word gets out and you can't hire
  more soldars, or the price goes way up.



